In article <28APR199316450851@gate.transalta.ab.ca>, 
trembath@gate.transalta.ab.ca (008039 G Lalonde) writes:

>We have several HP printers at our site and I can't stop them from printing
>blank pages before each and every job.  I've tried tried setting noburst
>and nofeed, but it didn't help. The reset module I'm using contains
>an <ESC>E sequence.

On August 5, 1992, Jerry Leichter <leichter@LRW.COM>, posted an excellent 
detailed explanation about this 'feature':

[...]
>	I've also noticed that the HP prints a blank page when switching to
>	landscape printing (I have done some testing) - is this normal?  Is it
>	avoidable?
>
>It's annoying, normal, and unavoidable.  The standard VMS printer symbiont
>wants to start each job at the top of an empty page.  It assumes that the
>data being sent to the printer consists of ANSI-style control sequences.  It
>assumes that those don't print anything, but that anything else does.
>
>Unfortunately, HP PCL does not conform to relevant ANSI standard, and PCL
>sequences almost never look like ANSI ones.  So the symbiont thinks the
>reset and setup modules have actually printed something, and inserts a
>form feed to resynchronize.
>
>If you look at the modules I've given above, you'll see that they each start
>with <ESC> P and end with <ESC> \.  Interpreted as ANSI sequences, these
>make the entire module a DCS, or Device Control String, thus hiding it from
>the symbiont.  You can get away with this because, at least as of the Laser-
>Jet II, neither sequence had any meaning in PCL, and the printer ignored it.
>(If strange things happen on your printer and it's more recent, check the
>documentation to see if these have since been given a meaning.  If so, you'll
>have to strip them out.)
>
>Without the "DCS hack", you'd get TWO blank sheets between jobs.  WITH it,
>you'll get ONE blank sheet.  Getting rid of that single blank sheet requires
>changes to the symbiont.  Modifications have been posted, but they are all
>fairly blunt instruments that disable more than just the printing of a single
>blank page; I've never used any of them.
[...]

